            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 29,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "types-of-access-modifiers",
      "heading": "Types of Access Modifiers",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Java has four types of access modifiers:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Keyword",
            "Applicable To",
            "Who Can Access"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "private",
              "Data members, methods, and inner classes",
              "Only within the same class"
            ],
            [
              "(default) (no keyword)",
              "Data members, methods, classes, and interfaces",
              "All classes within the same package"
            ],
            [
              "protected",
              "Data members, methods, and inner classes",
              "All classes within the same package and subclasses in other packages"
            ],
            [
              "public",
              "Data members, methods, classes, and interfaces",
              "Any class from any package"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 70,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "definitions",
          "heading": "Definitions",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Private: Limits access only to within the same class. ➔ Best for sensitive data (like passwords).",
                "Default (Package-Private): No access modifier specified. Accessible to other classes only within the same package.",
                "Protected: Accessible within the same package and in subclasses (even if subclass is in a different package).",
                "Public: No restrictions. Accessible from anywhere in the program across packages."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 59,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "why-use-access-modifiers",
      "heading": "Why Use Access Modifiers?",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Imagine an application where any class could modify sensitive data like a username or password of a Customer object:"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "class Customer {\n    String username;\n    String password;\n}",
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "❌ Unsafe: Anyone can modify username and password directly!",
            "✅ Safer with access modifiers:"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "class Customer {\n    private String username;\n    private String password;\n\n    public String getUsername() {\n        return username;\n    }\n\n    public void setUsername(String username) {\n        this.username = username;\n    }\n\n    public void setPassword(String password) {\n        this.password = password;\n    }\n}",
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Here, username and password are private.",
            "Only controlled access is allowed through public getter and setter methods."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 50,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "example",
      "heading": "Example",
      "level": 2,
